WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:01.303
- Hi, everyone,

00:00:01.303 --> 00:00:02.882
welcome to Homeroom with Sal.

00:00:02.882 --> 00:00:04.722
We have a very exciting conversation

00:00:04.722 --> 00:00:08.612
and I also have two
temporary co-hosts today

00:00:08.612 --> 00:00:11.192
because our guest is a
bit of a hero for them.

00:00:11.192 --> 00:00:13.202
We have a bit of a chess household.

00:00:13.202 --> 00:00:14.982
So we're going to have hopefully

00:00:14.982 --> 00:00:17.642
a very engaging conversation
with Magnus Carlsen,

00:00:17.642 --> 00:00:20.502
World Chess Champion,
highest rated chess player

00:00:20.502 --> 00:00:22.882
about all things chess and life

00:00:22.882 --> 00:00:25.532
and getting better at things
that might seem hard at first.

00:00:25.532 --> 00:00:27.612
But before we jump into that conversation

00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:30.042
I will give my standard announcements.

00:00:30.042 --> 00:00:30.875
First of all,

00:00:30.875 --> 00:00:32.482
reminder that Khan Academy
is a not-for-profit.

00:00:32.482 --> 00:00:34.772
We can only exist through
philanthropic donations.

00:00:34.772 --> 00:00:36.082
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:36.082 --> 00:00:40.302
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:40.302 --> 00:00:42.082
Also want to give a special shout out

00:00:42.082 --> 00:00:44.192
to a whole series of organizations

00:00:44.192 --> 00:00:45.542
that really helped step up

00:00:45.542 --> 00:00:48.172
when it was clear that
the world was depending

00:00:48.172 --> 00:00:50.782
on Khan Academy, more than
ever during the pandemic

00:00:50.782 --> 00:00:52.342
and we were running at a deficit.

00:00:52.342 --> 00:00:55.942
So special thanks to Bank of
America, AT&amp;T, Google.org,

00:00:55.942 --> 00:00:58.212
Novartis, Fastly and General Motors,

00:00:58.212 --> 00:01:00.612
and our many other
supporters of all levels,

00:01:00.612 --> 00:01:02.202
hundreds of thousands of folks

00:01:02.202 --> 00:01:03.752
who donate whatever they can

00:01:03.752 --> 00:01:06.322
to make Khan Academy a possibility.

00:01:06.322 --> 00:01:07.872
Also want to remind everyone

00:01:07.872 --> 00:01:10.962
that there is a podcast
version of this live stream

00:01:10.962 --> 00:01:12.432
wherever you get your podcasts,

00:01:12.432 --> 00:01:15.782
Homeroom with Sal The Podcast.

00:01:15.782 --> 00:01:19.592
So with that, I'm excited
to introduce Magnus,

00:01:19.592 --> 00:01:22.162
World Chess Champion.

00:01:22.162 --> 00:01:24.215
Magnus, great to have you here.

00:01:25.542 --> 00:01:26.972
- Thank you so much.

00:01:26.972 --> 00:01:27.855
Great to be here.

00:01:28.942 --> 00:01:30.682
- And this is a very special occasion.

00:01:30.682 --> 00:01:35.682
We've never had quite this
many co-hosts on the session.

00:01:36.842 --> 00:01:38.632
It's because of you.

00:01:38.632 --> 00:01:39.712
We're getting so many questions

00:01:39.712 --> 00:01:42.512
that are coming in over
social media and beyond.

00:01:42.512 --> 00:01:44.365
Maybe a good place to start is,

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:47.952
when you were young,

00:01:47.952 --> 00:01:51.132
when did you first realize that you had,

00:01:51.132 --> 00:01:52.402
for lack of a better word, a gift?

00:01:52.402 --> 00:01:54.912
That chess was something
that you were drawn to

00:01:54.912 --> 00:01:57.062
and that you might be
able to stand out in?

00:01:58.502 --> 00:02:00.182
- I think it sort of started

00:02:00.182 --> 00:02:03.485
when I was nine and a half years old.

00:02:05.052 --> 00:02:08.112
I started playing chess a little bit later

00:02:08.112 --> 00:02:11.502
than many of the other kids

00:02:11.502 --> 00:02:15.612
but I could notice that I
was sponging up information

00:02:19.592 --> 00:02:22.032
in a way that they were not.

00:02:22.032 --> 00:02:26.952
So like, I would play
tournaments with kids my age

00:02:26.952 --> 00:02:28.342
pretty much every weekend.

00:02:28.342 --> 00:02:32.302
And I could feel that I was
sort of improving week by week

00:02:33.752 --> 00:02:34.739
and they were not.

00:02:34.739 --> 00:02:39.217
And I realized that I had
just much more of a passion

00:02:39.217 --> 00:02:41.672
and a drive to do this than they had.

00:02:41.672 --> 00:02:44.232
So I think that's when I realized

00:02:44.232 --> 00:02:47.015
that I could actually
be very good at this.

00:02:48.142 --> 00:02:50.422
- And we have questions
coming in from Instagram.

00:02:50.422 --> 00:02:54.792
Creation asked, who taught
you how to play chess?

00:02:54.792 --> 00:02:56.112
How did you get introduced to the game?

00:02:56.112 --> 00:02:57.522
And you mentioned it was relatively late

00:02:57.522 --> 00:02:59.722
for someone who gets
to your level of chess.

00:03:00.712 --> 00:03:01.832
- Oh yeah, absolutely.

00:03:01.832 --> 00:03:06.832
So my father is quite a keen chess player.

00:03:06.902 --> 00:03:11.152
He still enjoys playing chess a lot.

00:03:11.152 --> 00:03:13.842
I would say he plays probably more chess

00:03:13.842 --> 00:03:15.715
than I do to be honest.

00:03:17.642 --> 00:03:22.642
He taught me and my sister
at a fairly young age.

00:03:23.782 --> 00:03:28.332
But up till the age I was eight,

00:03:28.332 --> 00:03:31.392
I really didn't know much at all.

00:03:31.392 --> 00:03:32.622
But certainly without him

00:03:32.622 --> 00:03:36.845
I would never have pursued
chess the way that I did.

00:03:37.732 --> 00:03:40.052
- And we're having a ton of
questions you could imagine

00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:43.652
from both experienced and
beginner chess players.

00:03:43.652 --> 00:03:45.682
From Instagram, Clara is asking,

00:03:45.682 --> 00:03:48.242
and I'm curious whether
you follow your own advice

00:03:48.242 --> 00:03:49.542
but what is the most important thing

00:03:49.542 --> 00:03:51.602
beginners should work on
to get better at chess?

00:03:51.602 --> 00:03:54.302
And what were you working
on when you were a beginner?

00:03:56.162 --> 00:03:57.222
- The thing is,

00:03:57.222 --> 00:04:01.295
I don't think I was working
on anything in particular.

00:04:02.952 --> 00:04:07.952
So we had some chess books
and magazines at home

00:04:11.762 --> 00:04:13.165
so I would read them.

00:04:15.293 --> 00:04:18.949
And there was this chess board we had.

00:04:20.422 --> 00:04:23.202
So I would move the
pieces around sometimes

00:04:23.202 --> 00:04:26.402
trying to imitate the games
that I had seen there.

00:04:26.402 --> 00:04:30.342
But like after learning the very basics

00:04:30.342 --> 00:04:32.502
and the rules from my father

00:04:32.502 --> 00:04:35.422
it was more really a case of me

00:04:35.422 --> 00:04:38.892
just trying out different things

00:04:38.892 --> 00:04:40.755
and seeing what worked.

00:04:43.284 --> 00:04:45.132
- You know, just as you
started playing other people

00:04:45.132 --> 00:04:46.182
it was a bit of a rocket ship.

00:04:46.182 --> 00:04:47.015
You just realized,

00:04:47.015 --> 00:04:48.082
hey, there's things you're processing

00:04:48.082 --> 00:04:48.915
that other people aren't,

00:04:48.915 --> 00:04:50.092
you're getting better faster.

00:04:50.092 --> 00:04:51.962
Were there times that you
were starting to plateau

00:04:51.962 --> 00:04:53.872
and you had to do certain
things to get out of that,

00:04:53.872 --> 00:04:55.072
to get to another level?

00:05:00.228 --> 00:05:02.132
- There were certainly
times when I was younger

00:05:02.132 --> 00:05:07.132
when yeah, I couldn't reach
that next level immediately.

00:05:07.932 --> 00:05:09.435
And in hindsight,

00:05:10.932 --> 00:05:14.875
obviously, that's something
that happens to everybody.

00:05:15.752 --> 00:05:18.482
What I believed back then is

00:05:18.482 --> 00:05:21.252
I had this sort of irrational confidence

00:05:21.252 --> 00:05:25.322
that these are temporary setbacks.

00:05:25.322 --> 00:05:26.682
Like the only reason

00:05:26.682 --> 00:05:28.512
why I'm not progressing to the next level

00:05:28.512 --> 00:05:31.522
is because I've had some bad luck

00:05:31.522 --> 00:05:33.532
or in this particular tournament,

00:05:33.532 --> 00:05:35.592
I wasn't able to show my best.

00:05:35.592 --> 00:05:38.142
So I always believed that
I'm going to pull through

00:05:38.142 --> 00:05:40.472
like next tournament, next game,

00:05:40.472 --> 00:05:42.035
that's going to be mine.

00:05:43.458 --> 00:05:47.082
And I think that mentality
more than anything else

00:05:47.082 --> 00:05:52.082
is what allowed me to push through.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:58.052
- And you know, I'm asking
you all of these questions

00:05:58.052 --> 00:05:59.762
because people might not realize

00:05:59.762 --> 00:06:01.202
I'm going to play a computerized

00:06:01.202 --> 00:06:04.662
10-year-old version of you in a few days.

00:06:04.662 --> 00:06:06.432
So I'm trying to get as
many tips as possible

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:07.765
to improve my chess game.

00:06:09.172 --> 00:06:11.012
Here's the little card about (mumbles)

00:06:11.012 --> 00:06:13.992
I'm going to be playing a
computerized 10-year-old Magnus.

00:06:13.992 --> 00:06:16.472
I'm a reasonably serious amateur,

00:06:16.472 --> 00:06:20.682
but I don't want to raise
anyone's expectations too much.

00:06:20.682 --> 00:06:23.582
And Magnus, you probably
don't remember this,

00:06:23.582 --> 00:06:26.412
I would like to say about
four or five years ago

00:06:26.412 --> 00:06:29.492
we actually played each other at an event.

00:06:29.492 --> 00:06:31.352
This was out here in Silicon Valley

00:06:31.352 --> 00:06:32.982
where I was randomly selected from a hat.

00:06:32.982 --> 00:06:34.512
I was one of four people to play you.

00:06:34.512 --> 00:06:35.482
You were blindfolded.

00:06:35.482 --> 00:06:37.052
I know you probably do this a lot

00:06:37.052 --> 00:06:39.065
but it was very memorable for me,

00:06:40.302 --> 00:06:42.782
and I was the last person standing.

00:06:42.782 --> 00:06:45.612
So that was an exciting game.

00:06:45.612 --> 00:06:49.264
- I was blindfolded (mumbles)

00:06:49.264 --> 00:06:50.914
Is that the one?

00:06:50.914 --> 00:06:53.925
Oh yeah, that I remember.

00:06:55.242 --> 00:06:58.155
- I'm sure that game you
remember vividly in your head as,

00:06:59.005 --> 00:07:01.755
(laughs loudly)

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:05.942
- Is that the game where I
forgot the position once.

00:07:05.942 --> 00:07:07.952
- Yes, you do remember that game.

00:07:07.952 --> 00:07:09.402
- Yeah.

00:07:09.402 --> 00:07:11.082
- I briefly had an advantage.

00:07:11.082 --> 00:07:12.163
I tell people that,

00:07:12.163 --> 00:07:13.482
they don't believe.
- I know.

00:07:13.482 --> 00:07:15.132
I think I blundered a rook or something.

00:07:15.132 --> 00:07:19.499
'Cause I misremembered one
of the pawns as far as I can.

00:07:19.499 --> 00:07:23.500
- Yeah, I was up one rook
at one point in the game.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.362
And I tell this to
everyone who would listen,

00:07:25.362 --> 00:07:29.112
I was at one point at an
advantage versus Magnus Carlsen.

00:07:29.112 --> 00:07:30.152
And then I later tell him

00:07:30.152 --> 00:07:32.522
that you were blindfolded
playing four other people.

00:07:32.522 --> 00:07:35.032
And I think you even might've
had less time on your clock

00:07:35.032 --> 00:07:36.412
than I did

00:07:36.412 --> 00:07:39.461
but it was good for my self esteem.

00:07:39.461 --> 00:07:40.752
I remember the moment when I saw that,

00:07:40.752 --> 00:07:43.472
I'm like, he's doing
something really deep,

00:07:43.472 --> 00:07:44.392
something really deep

00:07:44.392 --> 00:07:47.972
because I think I just
take this (indistinct)

00:07:48.932 --> 00:07:49.765
- No.

00:07:51.432 --> 00:07:54.852
Surprisingly often when you
see a very strong player

00:07:54.852 --> 00:07:58.142
appear to miss something of obvious,

00:07:58.142 --> 00:07:59.672
they just missed it.

00:07:59.672 --> 00:08:03.992
So that's what I keep telling everybody.

00:08:03.992 --> 00:08:06.822
If it looks like your
opponent missed something

00:08:06.822 --> 00:08:08.532
and you can't see why they didn't,

00:08:08.532 --> 00:08:09.615
they probably did.

00:08:10.452 --> 00:08:11.832
- That's actually a good life advice.

00:08:11.832 --> 00:08:13.522
'Cause I think all the time we questioned

00:08:13.522 --> 00:08:15.972
when there's something good happening.

00:08:15.972 --> 00:08:16.805
You know, I think this relates,

00:08:16.805 --> 00:08:19.192
actually, two of my kids

00:08:19.192 --> 00:08:20.782
who are very such serious chess players,

00:08:20.782 --> 00:08:22.192
this is Imran, and this is Azov,

00:08:22.192 --> 00:08:23.342
who have been hanging out here.

00:08:23.342 --> 00:08:24.492
Why don't y'all ask your questions.

00:08:24.492 --> 00:08:26.572
They're actually missing
schoolwork for this.

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:28.692
- I'm not missing schoolwork.

00:08:28.692 --> 00:08:29.525
- Okay

00:08:31.002 --> 00:08:32.002
- Ask your question Azov,

00:08:32.002 --> 00:08:33.032
then Imran you ask your question

00:08:33.032 --> 00:08:34.432
and then I'll follow up.

00:08:34.432 --> 00:08:36.237
What's your question, Azov?

00:08:36.237 --> 00:08:38.572
- How do you beat all the people?

00:08:38.572 --> 00:08:39.592
- How do you beat all the people?

00:08:39.592 --> 00:08:41.072
Okay, we're going to put
that in the (mumbles)

00:08:41.072 --> 00:08:43.552
Oh, well, do you have a response to that?

00:08:43.552 --> 00:08:45.102
How do you beat all the people?

00:08:47.632 --> 00:08:49.272
- I don't know, to be honest.

00:08:49.272 --> 00:08:51.495
And I don't beat all of them.

00:08:52.882 --> 00:08:55.902
More talent, harder work, who knows?

00:08:55.902 --> 00:08:56.742
- Talent and hard work.

00:08:56.742 --> 00:08:57.828
You hear that, Azov?

00:08:57.828 --> 00:08:58.730
- Okay.

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:01.032
- And Imran, what's your question.

00:09:01.032 --> 00:09:03.352
- So like when you play blindfolded,

00:09:03.352 --> 00:09:05.242
like you did against my dad,

00:09:05.242 --> 00:09:08.022
like, is there any specific
way that you can memorize

00:09:08.022 --> 00:09:10.782
the entire multiple boards in your head

00:09:10.782 --> 00:09:12.435
or do you just do it?

00:09:13.832 --> 00:09:15.402
- I think you can was sort of store them

00:09:15.402 --> 00:09:16.712
in different parts of your brain.

00:09:16.712 --> 00:09:20.022
So it's really only necessary

00:09:20.022 --> 00:09:23.482
to have one board at a time,

00:09:23.482 --> 00:09:28.482
but it's good to have some kind of face

00:09:30.792 --> 00:09:33.622
or some kind of name
that you connect with,

00:09:33.622 --> 00:09:34.572
with a certain board

00:09:34.572 --> 00:09:37.745
so it's easier to distinguish
them from each other.

00:09:39.192 --> 00:09:40.462
- Yeah, I mean, and
it's following actually

00:09:40.462 --> 00:09:41.812
on both of their questions.

00:09:42.992 --> 00:09:44.332
Famously and I think a lot of people,

00:09:44.332 --> 00:09:45.892
and you've commented on "Queens Gambit."

00:09:45.892 --> 00:09:47.742
A lot of people are really
getting into chess these days

00:09:47.742 --> 00:09:49.872
but it does seem like
people who can perform

00:09:49.872 --> 00:09:51.442
even close to your level

00:09:51.442 --> 00:09:53.972
seem to have almost superhuman memories.

00:09:53.972 --> 00:09:55.795
And it seems like you
do remember a lot of,

00:09:55.795 --> 00:09:58.252
I mean, the fact that you
remembered that game versus me,

00:09:58.252 --> 00:10:00.372
which is probably the least important game

00:10:00.372 --> 00:10:03.532
you've ever played, does speak to that.

00:10:03.532 --> 00:10:04.882
Is this something you've trained?

00:10:04.882 --> 00:10:05.792
Is it only in chess

00:10:05.792 --> 00:10:09.032
or do you have ability
to memorize scenarios

00:10:09.032 --> 00:10:11.332
and visualize things
outside of chess as well?

00:10:12.522 --> 00:10:14.352
- I would say that when I was young

00:10:14.352 --> 00:10:16.115
I had a very, very good memory.

00:10:18.972 --> 00:10:22.332
Now it's not so great anymore.

00:10:22.332 --> 00:10:23.572
I would say at this point,

00:10:23.572 --> 00:10:26.145
my memory is nothing exceptional.

00:10:28.934 --> 00:10:31.112
I can remember patterns still,

00:10:31.112 --> 00:10:33.992
I can remember the broad ideas

00:10:35.412 --> 00:10:37.292
but I kind of remember everything.

00:10:37.292 --> 00:10:38.532
I mean, let me tell you

00:10:38.532 --> 00:10:40.075
that when I was young,

00:10:43.442 --> 00:10:45.902
when I was a teenager, at
least in my early teens

00:10:45.902 --> 00:10:50.352
like there is no way that I would be able

00:10:51.872 --> 00:10:55.192
to only tell you that I
blundered rook at some point

00:10:55.192 --> 00:10:57.972
because I displaced my pawn.

00:11:01.842 --> 00:11:05.462
Back then I would've been able
to tell you the whole game

00:11:05.462 --> 00:11:08.602
and details about it.

00:11:08.602 --> 00:11:12.162
So I think now it's more

00:11:12.162 --> 00:11:13.175
as you get older,

00:11:16.779 --> 00:11:21.662
it's more about remembering
the broad lines of everything.

00:11:21.662 --> 00:11:26.105
And I don't think I'm a
real exceptional there.

00:11:27.462 --> 00:11:29.612
- And I think you're probably
being beat being humble

00:11:29.612 --> 00:11:30.495
but it is fascinating that you're saying

00:11:30.495 --> 00:11:33.712
that in some ways how
your brain tackles chess

00:11:33.712 --> 00:11:36.232
and you've stayed at the top
of your game this entire time

00:11:36.232 --> 00:11:38.522
but it has transitioned from being more

00:11:38.522 --> 00:11:40.632
I guess you could say memory intensive

00:11:40.632 --> 00:11:41.772
or computational intensive.

00:11:41.772 --> 00:11:44.832
And now it's more almost
pattern recognition

00:11:44.832 --> 00:11:45.672
is what I'm hearing.

00:11:45.672 --> 00:11:48.742
And there's a question
from Instagram, Brian

00:11:48.742 --> 00:11:49.972
it's related to this.

00:11:49.972 --> 00:11:51.532
Does being a world champion stop you

00:11:51.532 --> 00:11:54.032
from constantly trying
to improve your chess.

00:11:54.032 --> 00:11:56.005
How do you get better?

00:11:57.162 --> 00:11:59.700
Frankly, you're the best right now.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:01.595
How do you push yourself to be better?

00:12:03.792 --> 00:12:08.792
- Frankly, the emphasis
for me is on learning

00:12:09.332 --> 00:12:13.802
and I don't know exactly how that is going

00:12:13.802 --> 00:12:16.165
to make me a better chess player.

00:12:17.282 --> 00:12:22.282
But I feel as though my
understanding of chess is evolving.

00:12:25.132 --> 00:12:27.202
Looking at my games,

00:12:27.202 --> 00:12:29.622
let's say five, six years ago,

00:12:29.622 --> 00:12:33.922
I would say that my
understanding of the game

00:12:33.922 --> 00:12:37.782
was considerably worse back then.

00:12:37.782 --> 00:12:40.625
My results weren't necessarily worse.

00:12:42.352 --> 00:12:45.455
But that is the fascinating part for me,

00:12:47.185 --> 00:12:51.852
is that the game is evolving,

00:12:51.852 --> 00:12:54.242
my understanding is evolving

00:12:55.502 --> 00:13:00.272
and always learn learning something new.

00:13:00.272 --> 00:13:04.002
And that really is
enough motivation for me

00:13:04.002 --> 00:13:06.582
to keep on learning.

00:13:06.582 --> 00:13:09.525
Whether it will make me
a better chess player,

00:13:11.002 --> 00:13:12.152
I don't know.

00:13:12.152 --> 00:13:14.172
'Cause sometimes in chess,

00:13:14.172 --> 00:13:17.915
ignorance really can be bliss.

00:13:21.822 --> 00:13:24.772
Sometimes if you have less knowledge

00:13:24.772 --> 00:13:27.682
but knowledge that you are confident about

00:13:27.682 --> 00:13:32.682
it can be better than just
having many, many ideas

00:13:33.862 --> 00:13:38.479
many, many thoughts that
just makes you overthink.

00:13:42.432 --> 00:13:43.265
- That was fascinating.

00:13:43.265 --> 00:13:45.922
I mean, I'm really actually
blown away by the comment

00:13:45.922 --> 00:13:47.632
where you say that four or five years ago

00:13:47.632 --> 00:13:50.152
your understanding of the
game was much less deep

00:13:50.152 --> 00:13:50.985
than it is today,

00:13:50.985 --> 00:13:51.818
because four or five years ago

00:13:51.818 --> 00:13:53.952
you were already World Chess Champion.

00:13:53.952 --> 00:13:54.785
And you're saying that

00:13:54.785 --> 00:13:58.542
you had a more superficial
understanding of the game.

00:13:58.542 --> 00:14:00.382
So, I mean, it's fascinating
that there's still layers

00:14:00.382 --> 00:14:01.482
that you are uncovering

00:14:01.482 --> 00:14:03.532
even after becoming World Chess Champion.

00:14:04.542 --> 00:14:06.572
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:06.572 --> 00:14:11.482
And I think that happens
to all chess players

00:14:11.482 --> 00:14:14.012
because the game is evolving

00:14:14.012 --> 00:14:19.012
and it's the tragedy for everybody that

00:14:20.372 --> 00:14:21.555
the more you know,

00:14:22.602 --> 00:14:24.672
the worse you play

00:14:24.672 --> 00:14:25.815
becomes at some point.

00:14:27.352 --> 00:14:28.822
- And I'm curious,

00:14:28.822 --> 00:14:31.972
as someone who is an amateur chess player

00:14:31.972 --> 00:14:33.772
but I'm sure even many
experienced chess players.

00:14:33.772 --> 00:14:35.255
What goes on in your head?

00:14:36.182 --> 00:14:37.662
You know, in a lot of chess

00:14:37.662 --> 00:14:40.242
someone like me, I might
recognize certain things

00:14:40.242 --> 00:14:42.452
but I'm also doing a lot of the scenarios.

00:14:42.452 --> 00:14:44.052
Well, if I do this,
they're going to do this.

00:14:44.052 --> 00:14:45.592
If I do this, they're going to do this.

00:14:45.592 --> 00:14:47.252
And I can go to down that tree

00:14:47.252 --> 00:14:49.385
maybe three or four or five moves at best.

00:14:50.402 --> 00:14:51.912
Are you doing that same type of thing

00:14:51.912 --> 00:14:53.512
wait, if I do this, they do that

00:14:53.512 --> 00:14:55.375
or is it something else?

00:14:57.182 --> 00:14:58.355
- Oh, it can be.

00:15:00.902 --> 00:15:05.412
But I think, since I'm
much more experienced

00:15:05.412 --> 00:15:09.482
I would use my long-term memory

00:15:09.482 --> 00:15:11.635
more than my short term.

00:15:12.802 --> 00:15:17.382
But clearly, sometimes
you do have to calculate

00:15:17.382 --> 00:15:22.262
and then it's, as you say, no deeper than,

00:15:22.262 --> 00:15:25.192
yeah, if I do this, you
will have to do this

00:15:25.192 --> 00:15:27.572
and then I'll do that and so on.

00:15:27.572 --> 00:15:32.572
And then there may be two or
three options on each move.

00:15:34.182 --> 00:15:36.312
So the tree of variation

00:15:36.312 --> 00:15:40.022
sort of becomes broader and messier.

00:15:40.022 --> 00:15:42.632
But at the end of the day

00:15:42.632 --> 00:15:47.632
you can't escape sort of
calculation by brute force

00:15:50.012 --> 00:15:53.332
no matter how much you want to play

00:15:53.332 --> 00:15:57.222
on intuition and strategy
rather than tactics

00:15:58.312 --> 00:15:59.500
- I can imagine.

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:01.002
And we have a question here
from Facebook, Margo McGinnis

00:16:01.002 --> 00:16:04.052
who actually helped coordinate
the first time we played

00:16:04.052 --> 00:16:05.412
and now this time that
I'm going to be playing

00:16:05.412 --> 00:16:06.725
versus the digital you.

00:16:08.102 --> 00:16:09.342
This is Margo's question.

00:16:09.342 --> 00:16:11.802
What was your most memorable
or nerve-racking match?

00:16:11.802 --> 00:16:13.212
And I'll add to this,

00:16:13.212 --> 00:16:14.152
how do you deal with stress?

00:16:14.152 --> 00:16:15.852
Do you get nervous during matches?

00:16:18.132 --> 00:16:19.642
- Yeah, I would certainly say

00:16:20.627 --> 00:16:25.627
the first World Championship
match that I had in 2013,

00:16:27.872 --> 00:16:30.112
at the start, I was so nervous

00:16:30.112 --> 00:16:33.775
that I dropped one of my
pieces on the first move,

00:16:38.852 --> 00:16:41.072
which is so fascinating

00:16:42.402 --> 00:16:44.162
because even at that point

00:16:44.162 --> 00:16:46.122
I was the highest rated
player in the world

00:16:46.122 --> 00:16:47.632
and I was very experienced.

00:16:47.632 --> 00:16:50.108
But the task of playing
the World Championship

00:16:50.108 --> 00:16:51.952
was so daunting to me

00:16:54.212 --> 00:16:56.852
that for the first four games

00:16:56.852 --> 00:16:58.582
or yeah, three or four games,

00:16:58.582 --> 00:17:01.822
I was still unbelievably nervous

00:17:01.822 --> 00:17:04.522
'cause it was something that
was absolutely new to me.

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:06.742
- And how do you deal with that?

00:17:06.742 --> 00:17:08.962
Do you just say, breathe,

00:17:08.962 --> 00:17:10.745
one move at a time kind of thing?

00:17:13.372 --> 00:17:16.252
- I don't know how to deal
with it, to be honest.

00:17:16.252 --> 00:17:19.212
So the way that I dealt with it was

00:17:21.572 --> 00:17:22.812
I played one good game

00:17:22.812 --> 00:17:25.172
and then my confidence was back

00:17:25.172 --> 00:17:27.705
and I didn't have a care in the world.

00:17:31.992 --> 00:17:36.812
Very often people search for like

00:17:36.812 --> 00:17:41.082
do I have any method or
deeper meaning to what I do

00:17:41.082 --> 00:17:45.032
but what I explained at the beginning was,

00:17:45.032 --> 00:17:47.282
my method was mainly trial and error.

00:17:47.282 --> 00:17:51.082
And it still is to some extent.

00:17:51.082 --> 00:17:56.082
That I find out what
works for me at some point

00:17:56.742 --> 00:18:01.252
simply by experiencing it,

00:18:01.252 --> 00:18:03.585
not necessarily by good planning.

00:18:04.822 --> 00:18:07.022
- And do you ever while you're
in the middle of the game

00:18:07.022 --> 00:18:07.902
(mumbles)

00:18:07.902 --> 00:18:09.912
We always be, oh, I can't
believe I made that move.

00:18:09.912 --> 00:18:11.042
Like that was a (indistinct)

00:18:11.042 --> 00:18:12.302
How often does that happen to you?

00:18:12.302 --> 00:18:13.332
That you're like, oh my god,

00:18:13.332 --> 00:18:14.722
that was a careless mistake,

00:18:14.722 --> 00:18:16.212
or I can't believe I did that

00:18:17.562 --> 00:18:18.775
- Every single game.

00:18:19.799 --> 00:18:22.466
(laughs loudly)

00:18:23.322 --> 00:18:25.462
Yeah, I can't remember a single one

00:18:29.622 --> 00:18:32.112
where I haven't had those thoughts.

00:18:32.112 --> 00:18:35.145
Like sometimes there would
be a massive blunder,

00:18:37.544 --> 00:18:40.902
another time it will be
an unavoidable inaccuracy.

00:18:42.712 --> 00:18:44.345
But every time it's like,

00:18:45.612 --> 00:18:47.172
you've played chess your whole life

00:18:47.172 --> 00:18:48.972
and still you can't figure this out.

00:18:50.833 --> 00:18:51.865
Yeah, it's the same.

00:18:53.172 --> 00:18:54.432
- And it sounds like,

00:18:54.432 --> 00:18:56.432
to your earlier comment about that moment

00:18:56.432 --> 00:18:58.112
which I will never stop bragging about

00:18:58.112 --> 00:19:00.502
where I had an advantage
versus you playing blindfolded

00:19:00.502 --> 00:19:01.802
against four other people.

00:19:03.282 --> 00:19:04.572
Is what you said earlier,

00:19:04.572 --> 00:19:06.092
I mean, you probably hide it well

00:19:06.092 --> 00:19:09.732
because now what you
know is to be a mistake

00:19:09.732 --> 00:19:10.662
or you miss something,

00:19:10.662 --> 00:19:15.152
most other people will probably
see it as a deep insight.

00:19:15.152 --> 00:19:18.585
Something that it probably scares them.

00:19:19.682 --> 00:19:23.465
- Yeah, normally it's just a mess up.

00:19:24.622 --> 00:19:27.082
That's what it is.

00:19:27.082 --> 00:19:29.072
- You know, there's a lot
of questions coming in

00:19:29.072 --> 00:19:31.932
about chess and your life.

00:19:31.932 --> 00:19:33.712
You know, I guess this is a question.

00:19:33.712 --> 00:19:36.312
YouTube Ghost Tommy, how's
chess affected your life?

00:19:37.888 --> 00:19:38.955
Has it improved you?

00:19:39.852 --> 00:19:42.682
And I'll extend that question
of how are you viewing chess

00:19:42.682 --> 00:19:43.832
and your life going forward?

00:19:43.832 --> 00:19:46.962
And you've achieved the
top of your game literally

00:19:46.962 --> 00:19:49.072
at a very, very young age.

00:19:49.072 --> 00:19:50.642
You're still very, very young.

00:19:50.642 --> 00:19:52.112
What do you see as the future?

00:19:52.112 --> 00:19:54.288
Do you feel, you know,
defending your title?

00:19:54.288 --> 00:19:55.592
(mumbles)

00:19:55.592 --> 00:19:57.002
You're constantly on your game

00:19:57.002 --> 00:19:58.842
or is it really just the love of the game

00:19:58.842 --> 00:20:01.065
and what else is going
on outside of chess?

00:20:02.582 --> 00:20:06.782
- No, really I just want to learn

00:20:08.592 --> 00:20:13.562
and playing both
tournaments and casual games

00:20:16.143 --> 00:20:19.212
still give me satisfaction and joy.

00:20:19.212 --> 00:20:21.862
So for the moment,

00:20:21.862 --> 00:20:25.875
I haven't really thought too
much about anything else.

00:20:28.032 --> 00:20:32.272
Whatever titles maybe out there

00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:36.555
that's not the main
motivation at this point.

00:20:38.512 --> 00:20:41.705
In that case, I feel like
it's just calm with me.

00:20:46.427 --> 00:20:49.642
It's about learning and
having fun, to be honest.

00:20:50.682 --> 00:20:51.515
- It's good to hear that.

00:20:51.515 --> 00:20:53.772
I mean, it's amazing
when you even said that

00:20:53.772 --> 00:20:56.492
how much more you've learned
about the game of chess

00:20:56.492 --> 00:20:57.762
over the last four or five years

00:20:57.762 --> 00:20:59.322
when you were already
at the top of your game.

00:20:59.322 --> 00:21:00.982
It really does sound like
there's this opportunity,

00:21:00.982 --> 00:21:01.815
there's a journey.

00:21:01.815 --> 00:21:05.332
There's a beauty in the
game that never gets old.

00:21:05.332 --> 00:21:06.165
And related to that,

00:21:06.165 --> 00:21:08.812
there's a question here from
Facebook, Cynthia Hurtado,

00:21:08.812 --> 00:21:10.012
and this is a good question,

00:21:10.012 --> 00:21:11.542
something I've always wondered.

00:21:11.542 --> 00:21:13.552
What do you think about
the rise of chess engines

00:21:13.552 --> 00:21:15.802
and the digital age of chess?

00:21:15.802 --> 00:21:17.122
How has that changed the game?

00:21:17.122 --> 00:21:18.852
How has that changed your thinking?

00:21:18.852 --> 00:21:20.952
Is it different to play
against a supercomputer

00:21:20.952 --> 00:21:23.055
versus a great human player?

00:21:25.832 --> 00:21:29.345
- Frankly, I don't play
a lot against computers.

00:21:30.482 --> 00:21:32.902
Like basically the only time

00:21:32.902 --> 00:21:34.632
that I play against computer opponents

00:21:34.632 --> 00:21:36.855
is when I play my own app.

00:21:38.805 --> 00:21:43.805
And computers have changed dramatically

00:21:46.032 --> 00:21:49.885
the way that we prepare for games.

00:21:51.772 --> 00:21:55.022
Not necessarily for the better

00:21:55.022 --> 00:22:00.022
because it's left sort of
less scope for creativity.

00:22:03.362 --> 00:22:07.052
I think computers though
have made the game

00:22:07.052 --> 00:22:09.602
much more accessible for casual fans

00:22:09.602 --> 00:22:12.035
because now you can sort of see,

00:22:12.892 --> 00:22:14.002
you can see a score

00:22:14.002 --> 00:22:18.592
and they can also aid in
giving you instant feedback

00:22:18.592 --> 00:22:21.182
when you play your games.

00:22:21.182 --> 00:22:23.065
So I guess overall,

00:22:24.012 --> 00:22:28.112
they've certainly been a positive

00:22:28.112 --> 00:22:33.112
but in terms of how I view chess engines,

00:22:35.562 --> 00:22:39.525
I view them as more of a,

00:22:40.972 --> 00:22:45.515
for me, a necessary
evil than a good thing.

00:22:46.802 --> 00:22:47.935
To me, it would be,

00:22:51.912 --> 00:22:54.682
yeah, the game would
be just as interesting

00:22:54.682 --> 00:22:56.995
without a computer analysis.

00:22:58.042 --> 00:23:01.132
- And you know, you said
a really interesting thing

00:23:01.132 --> 00:23:02.782
that I've heard great chess players say,

00:23:02.782 --> 00:23:04.232
you know, the creativity of the game,

00:23:04.232 --> 00:23:05.082
the beauty of the game.

00:23:05.082 --> 00:23:07.002
And I definitely have glimpses of that

00:23:07.002 --> 00:23:07.862
when I sometimes play.

00:23:07.862 --> 00:23:10.102
But double-click on that a little bit.

00:23:10.102 --> 00:23:11.672
What do you mean where it says,

00:23:11.672 --> 00:23:13.682
when you play against human versus human,

00:23:13.682 --> 00:23:16.722
there's maybe a little
more space for creativity?

00:23:16.722 --> 00:23:17.722
What does that mean?

00:23:19.473 --> 00:23:21.722
- It just means that humans

00:23:24.612 --> 00:23:26.442
they have emotions,

00:23:26.442 --> 00:23:28.595
they have actual thoughts.

00:23:33.133 --> 00:23:36.682
It's sort of a game of both
some emotion and ideas.

00:23:40.242 --> 00:23:41.832
And with computers,

00:23:41.832 --> 00:23:46.832
it just becomes technique basically.

00:23:46.852 --> 00:23:49.092
It's only about technique

00:23:49.092 --> 00:23:53.012
and that's why I find it in
general, less interesting

00:23:53.992 --> 00:23:55.292
- Is that part of your game?

00:23:55.292 --> 00:23:56.392
I mean, I find it fascinating.

00:23:56.392 --> 00:23:59.242
You know, chess famously
was built off of a metaphor

00:23:59.242 --> 00:24:02.732
for real warfare in the real
world, an ancient warfare.

00:24:02.732 --> 00:24:04.082
And ancient warfare we all know

00:24:04.082 --> 00:24:06.672
isn't just who is the
best tactical fighter.

00:24:06.672 --> 00:24:10.185
There's psychology, there's
tricks, there's faints.

00:24:13.256 --> 00:24:16.342
How much of that is a game
at the very highest level?

00:24:16.342 --> 00:24:19.402
I do imagine these people who
are playing with each other

00:24:19.402 --> 00:24:20.832
they're thinking way down the road,

00:24:20.832 --> 00:24:22.182
they can think about the positions,

00:24:22.182 --> 00:24:23.362
they can think about all these things.

00:24:23.362 --> 00:24:26.372
How much is it playing with
the other person's emotions

00:24:26.372 --> 00:24:27.272
or tricking them

00:24:27.272 --> 00:24:29.482
or making them not see something,

00:24:29.482 --> 00:24:31.782
kind of do a faint of
hands, things like that?

00:24:39.542 --> 00:24:41.402
- The faster the games,

00:24:41.402 --> 00:24:46.162
the more there's room
for all sorts of trickery

00:24:48.152 --> 00:24:53.152
that to my mind makes the
game more interesting.

00:24:58.272 --> 00:25:00.032
Again, as you have more and more time,

00:25:00.032 --> 00:25:04.132
it becomes more technical.

00:25:04.132 --> 00:25:09.132
But certainly at shorter time controls,

00:25:09.152 --> 00:25:14.152
there is a lot of
psychology involved in it.

00:25:14.672 --> 00:25:16.302
There's much more room

00:25:18.748 --> 00:25:22.585
to sort of adjust your strategy
according to your opponents.

00:25:29.118 --> 00:25:30.512
- I think a lot of people
have watched "Queens Gambit."

00:25:30.512 --> 00:25:31.622
I've seen on social media

00:25:31.622 --> 00:25:34.092
you've commented on
"Queens Gambit" a good bit.

00:25:34.092 --> 00:25:36.122
I mean, I am curious in this forum,

00:25:36.122 --> 00:25:37.592
what are your thoughts about it?

00:25:37.592 --> 00:25:39.052
And do you think it was pretty realistic

00:25:39.052 --> 00:25:40.132
to the game of chess?

00:25:40.132 --> 00:25:41.682
The main character in "Queens Gambit"

00:25:41.682 --> 00:25:44.732
she famously is kind
of an intuitive player

00:25:44.732 --> 00:25:47.082
versus a kind of a computational.

00:25:47.082 --> 00:25:48.172
I'm sure she's doing some of that too

00:25:48.172 --> 00:25:49.882
or at least the character would.

00:25:49.882 --> 00:25:50.962
Well, what are your thoughts about it

00:25:50.962 --> 00:25:52.962
and what's it done to the game of chess?

00:25:54.662 --> 00:25:57.192
- Well, I think the only
reason she can be intuitive

00:25:57.192 --> 00:25:59.945
is that she studied very, very hard.

00:26:02.932 --> 00:26:03.765
One of the things

00:26:03.765 --> 00:26:06.892
I love to see about the series,

00:26:06.892 --> 00:26:11.362
is that she studies so hard

00:26:11.362 --> 00:26:14.455
and she has such great
respect for the game.

00:26:15.602 --> 00:26:20.602
Also, I feel like they did
the chess pretty well there.

00:26:22.702 --> 00:26:27.505
The fragments from games
were very realistic

00:26:30.671 --> 00:26:32.445
and sometimes beautiful as well.

00:26:34.752 --> 00:26:37.132
When it comes to the atmosphere

00:26:37.132 --> 00:26:38.622
around the tournaments and so on,

00:26:38.622 --> 00:26:40.992
I guess some things are fictionalized.

00:26:40.992 --> 00:26:45.052
But overall, I was very happy with it

00:26:45.052 --> 00:26:49.662
and hopefully people will watch it

00:26:49.662 --> 00:26:52.102
and they will not be scared,

00:26:52.102 --> 00:26:57.102
but rather encouraged to play chess.

00:26:58.732 --> 00:26:59.902
- And you know, there's so many questions.

00:26:59.902 --> 00:27:01.032
I know we're running low on time

00:27:01.032 --> 00:27:02.892
but I'll ask a few more of these,

00:27:02.892 --> 00:27:05.152
really around how do you cope with

00:27:05.152 --> 00:27:06.042
especially in the early days

00:27:06.042 --> 00:27:07.802
when maybe you were getting
beaten every now and then.

00:27:07.802 --> 00:27:10.032
How did you deal with that failure

00:27:10.032 --> 00:27:12.402
and how do you manage your own emotions?

00:27:12.402 --> 00:27:13.962
I mean, are there times that you get

00:27:13.962 --> 00:27:17.762
where you're feeling either
bad about yourself or angry

00:27:17.762 --> 00:27:20.065
or you're frustrated
with a given opponent?

00:27:22.002 --> 00:27:24.712
- I have to say that when I was young

00:27:24.712 --> 00:27:29.245
and I guess I lost a
lot more than I do now,

00:27:31.242 --> 00:27:33.192
I didn't care so much about losses

00:27:33.192 --> 00:27:36.695
'cause I didn't expect
to win all the time.

00:27:37.592 --> 00:27:39.332
As I said, it was like,

00:27:39.332 --> 00:27:41.092
I wanted to play stronger players

00:27:41.092 --> 00:27:42.825
that I could learn the most.

00:27:44.652 --> 00:27:45.532
It was not about winning.

00:27:45.532 --> 00:27:50.532
I just wanted to sort of
get to the next level,

00:27:54.272 --> 00:27:57.992
that I could match up with
stronger and stronger opponents

00:27:57.992 --> 00:28:00.235
and then beat them eventually.

00:28:02.282 --> 00:28:03.272
In recent years

00:28:03.272 --> 00:28:07.995
when I've become used to being
at the top most of the time,

00:28:09.122 --> 00:28:13.822
yeah, now losing hurts.

00:28:13.822 --> 00:28:16.522
I'm not gonna lie there, like every time.

00:28:17.382 --> 00:28:18.892
And it's not even losing.

00:28:18.892 --> 00:28:23.732
Like I might be equally
upset after a game that I won

00:28:23.732 --> 00:28:25.242
as a game that I lost

00:28:25.242 --> 00:28:28.015
if I feel like I did something,

00:28:28.972 --> 00:28:33.130
I made a mistake that I
could easily have avoided

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:35.292
or I didn't act professionally enough

00:28:35.292 --> 00:28:38.035
in the way that I executed the game.

00:28:38.992 --> 00:28:43.992
So yeah, 20 years of playing
chess has not prevented me

00:28:46.102 --> 00:28:50.402
from having sort of emotional reactions

00:28:50.402 --> 00:28:53.375
to things that happen at the chess board.

00:28:55.152 --> 00:28:57.262
- You know, one of the
things that I think is hard

00:28:57.262 --> 00:28:59.725
when people have been so
successful for so long,

00:29:00.682 --> 00:29:02.682
do you sometimes model in your mind,

00:29:02.682 --> 00:29:05.142
what if five years, 10 years in the future

00:29:05.142 --> 00:29:06.722
the next Magnus Carlsen comes

00:29:06.722 --> 00:29:08.432
and is able to beat me?

00:29:08.432 --> 00:29:10.222
Is that something that you think about?

00:29:10.222 --> 00:29:14.405
How do you think that would
affect you if that happened?

00:29:16.622 --> 00:29:19.635
- I remember I was asked this question,

00:29:21.297 --> 00:29:23.362
I think 11 years ago.

00:29:23.362 --> 00:29:26.322
And at that point it upset me

00:29:26.322 --> 00:29:29.852
because they worded the question

00:29:30.822 --> 00:29:35.822
as what are you going to do
when that next one comes along?

00:29:36.962 --> 00:29:41.962
And I said very clear that, in my mind

00:29:42.732 --> 00:29:44.215
this was a question of if.

00:29:47.182 --> 00:29:51.272
And also that I thought
I have so many years

00:29:51.272 --> 00:29:54.955
till that will be a real thing.

00:29:56.232 --> 00:29:59.972
I think I'm realistic
enough though at this point

00:29:59.972 --> 00:30:03.022
to realize that I'm not going to be able

00:30:03.022 --> 00:30:05.635
to be at the highest level forever.

00:30:10.275 --> 00:30:11.729
If I do want to keep on playing
chess at the highest level,

00:30:14.562 --> 00:30:18.162
then at some point I'm
going to start regressing.

00:30:20.642 --> 00:30:24.085
But for the moment,

00:30:25.232 --> 00:30:26.562
I'm thinking like

00:30:26.562 --> 00:30:30.502
I should still should
still be in my prime,

00:30:30.502 --> 00:30:32.615
and that's what I should strive towards.

00:30:33.622 --> 00:30:35.272
- Yeah, no, I definitely phrased it as if.

00:30:35.272 --> 00:30:37.365
I'm not assuming that anyone is gonna,

00:30:38.209 --> 00:30:39.042
(indistinct)

00:30:39.042 --> 00:30:42.612
- No, obviously the most
likely scenario is that

00:30:42.612 --> 00:30:44.835
somebody is going to surpass me,

00:30:47.042 --> 00:30:49.072
I wouldn't say sooner rather than later,

00:30:49.072 --> 00:30:52.822
but in the not too distant future.

00:30:52.822 --> 00:30:55.972
But I'm gonna try

00:30:55.972 --> 00:30:57.585
and hold on as long as I can.

00:31:00.372 --> 00:31:02.452
- When we think about the
arc of the game of chess,

00:31:02.452 --> 00:31:03.622
how it's evolved,

00:31:03.622 --> 00:31:06.722
do you think that the
competition is only going higher

00:31:06.722 --> 00:31:08.152
and higher and higher over time,

00:31:08.152 --> 00:31:09.592
that people are understanding the game

00:31:09.592 --> 00:31:10.712
at deeper and deeper levels?

00:31:10.712 --> 00:31:13.502
Or do you think some of the
old greats from 50 years ago

00:31:13.502 --> 00:31:15.962
or 60 years ago, or even 100 years ago

00:31:15.962 --> 00:31:17.762
would actually still be great today?

00:31:19.302 --> 00:31:20.875
- I think it's a bit of both.

00:31:21.892 --> 00:31:24.192
The game is certainly evolving

00:31:24.192 --> 00:31:26.409
especially with the computers.

00:31:26.409 --> 00:31:29.232
It has made us reevaluate

00:31:29.232 --> 00:31:34.232
a lot of the truth that
we held to be self-evident

00:31:37.322 --> 00:31:38.385
once upon a time.

00:31:40.102 --> 00:31:42.542
But I also do feel,

00:31:42.542 --> 00:31:47.542
and this is gonna make me
sound a lot older than I am.

00:31:47.892 --> 00:31:52.842
But I do feel that some
of the youngsters today

00:31:54.902 --> 00:31:58.072
they're not too good at the basics.

00:31:58.072 --> 00:32:03.072
Like, I feel like the generation

00:32:03.072 --> 00:32:08.072
that I'm a part of and
the generation before

00:32:08.162 --> 00:32:11.632
maybe understood the
fundamentals of the game

00:32:11.632 --> 00:32:15.512
a little bit better
than the kids do today.

00:32:15.512 --> 00:32:19.022
And I think that's partly

00:32:19.022 --> 00:32:24.022
a result of growing up with a computer

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:32.085
rather than a board.

00:32:34.162 --> 00:32:34.995
- It's fascinating.

00:32:34.995 --> 00:32:36.747
'Cause I'm sure the youngsters
that you're talking about

00:32:36.747 --> 00:32:38.892
are still quite good at chess.

00:32:38.892 --> 00:32:39.852
But you're saying,

00:32:39.852 --> 00:32:40.965
- Obviously yes.

00:32:42.192 --> 00:32:43.182
- even some of these folks,

00:32:43.182 --> 00:32:44.042
and when you say the basics

00:32:44.042 --> 00:32:44.892
what do you mean by that?

00:32:44.892 --> 00:32:46.042
Like what types of things

00:32:46.042 --> 00:32:48.142
do you think they have maybe some gaps in?

00:32:49.072 --> 00:32:53.312
- I think some gaps in just

00:32:53.312 --> 00:32:56.595
general positional
understanding of the game.

00:33:02.661 --> 00:33:04.752
Like studying the classic games

00:33:06.242 --> 00:33:09.172
I think has clear value.

00:33:09.172 --> 00:33:14.015
Like far from everything
that you learn from back then

00:33:15.012 --> 00:33:17.512
is still true today.

00:33:17.512 --> 00:33:21.292
But I don't think you can develop

00:33:21.292 --> 00:33:26.292
a broad enough understanding
of the game to be the best

00:33:29.112 --> 00:33:31.135
by only training with a computer.

00:33:32.252 --> 00:33:33.212
- It's fascinating.

00:33:33.212 --> 00:33:35.502
Yes, you do sound older than you are.

00:33:35.502 --> 00:33:37.192
How old are you exactly?

00:33:37.192 --> 00:33:38.802
(mumbles)

00:33:38.802 --> 00:33:43.295
- Yeah, I just turned 30 last November.

00:33:45.112 --> 00:33:46.412
What I will say though,

00:33:46.412 --> 00:33:47.312
I think I'm lucky

00:33:47.312 --> 00:33:50.265
to be in sort of a
hybrid generation there.

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:53.992
I sort of started playing chess

00:33:57.052 --> 00:34:00.512
before the computers were
absolutely everywhere.

00:34:00.512 --> 00:34:04.792
Like I actually went
to a school with books

00:34:06.512 --> 00:34:09.102
and notebooks and so on

00:34:09.102 --> 00:34:12.402
before everything became
computerized and so on.

00:34:12.402 --> 00:34:15.672
And I think it's sort of a good position

00:34:15.672 --> 00:34:18.602
to be in that sort of a hybrid

00:34:18.602 --> 00:34:22.572
that you have a good
knowledge of computers

00:34:22.572 --> 00:34:26.042
but that you can also sort of relate

00:34:26.042 --> 00:34:31.042
to the old way the game was played.

00:34:32.381 --> 00:34:34.242
- And maybe in the time we have left,

00:34:34.242 --> 00:34:35.912
you know, one question that
I'm sure a lot of people,

00:34:35.912 --> 00:34:37.422
and people are asking this question,

00:34:37.422 --> 00:34:40.242
what other subjects are you
interested in, Math or physics?

00:34:40.242 --> 00:34:42.032
There's sometimes
connection between chess.

00:34:42.032 --> 00:34:42.902
But I am curious

00:34:42.902 --> 00:34:45.822
have you ever done the thought exercise?

00:34:45.822 --> 00:34:47.402
If you weren't World Chess Champion

00:34:47.402 --> 00:34:49.412
or if you didn't have this chess life,

00:34:49.412 --> 00:34:50.622
in that alternate reality

00:34:50.622 --> 00:34:52.395
what would Magnus Carlsen be doing?

00:34:53.782 --> 00:34:58.162
- Yeah, I've been asked
it many times for sure.

00:34:58.162 --> 00:35:00.172
Never had a good answer,

00:35:00.172 --> 00:35:02.882
probably still don't.

00:35:02.882 --> 00:35:05.602
One thing that I will say though

00:35:05.602 --> 00:35:08.882
is that I have massive respect

00:35:09.892 --> 00:35:14.892
for people in almost any field.

00:35:16.332 --> 00:35:21.332
And I think there are lots
even fairly bright people

00:35:22.742 --> 00:35:26.352
who think that somebody like me

00:35:26.352 --> 00:35:29.905
could become good at something
else with very little effort.

00:35:31.830 --> 00:35:34.882
But I really don't think so.

00:35:34.882 --> 00:35:39.805
I think most of all, I'm somebody who

00:35:42.442 --> 00:35:45.032
evidently, I have some talent for chess,

00:35:45.032 --> 00:35:45.865
but most of all,

00:35:45.865 --> 00:35:48.482
I've spent a lot of time working on it

00:35:48.482 --> 00:35:50.682
and thinking about it.

00:35:50.682 --> 00:35:53.872
And I think that I'm not necessarily

00:35:55.322 --> 00:35:58.675
the fastest at anything.

00:35:59.762 --> 00:36:01.713
I need some time.

00:36:01.713 --> 00:36:05.805
and then I just keep learning.

00:36:07.112 --> 00:36:09.985
But to think of something else,

00:36:11.142 --> 00:36:14.302
no, I don't think like there's anything

00:36:17.216 --> 00:36:21.569
that I could imagine I would
be anywhere equally as good at.

00:36:22.812 --> 00:36:23.645
In that case

00:36:23.645 --> 00:36:26.552
I would have to spend
nearly all my time on it

00:36:26.552 --> 00:36:28.225
as I have with chess.

00:36:29.342 --> 00:36:30.602
- Well, it does seem like the mindset

00:36:30.602 --> 00:36:32.421
that you've taken to chess.

00:36:32.421 --> 00:36:33.572
There's probably things your mind can do

00:36:33.572 --> 00:36:35.532
that would be very useful in other fields

00:36:35.532 --> 00:36:37.189
even if we limit ourselves to games.

00:36:37.189 --> 00:36:39.322
Do you play other games?

00:36:39.322 --> 00:36:42.032
You know, famously a lot
of elite athletes who,

00:36:42.032 --> 00:36:43.692
you know, Michael Jordan tried golf,

00:36:43.692 --> 00:36:46.302
LeBron James, I recently
read an article of like

00:36:46.302 --> 00:36:47.772
he could have been a football player.

00:36:47.772 --> 00:36:48.605
Have you thought,

00:36:48.605 --> 00:36:50.272
well, there's other games
I could have played,

00:36:50.272 --> 00:36:52.675
Go or Mahjong or something else.

00:36:54.152 --> 00:36:55.522
Have you ever tried these things

00:36:55.522 --> 00:36:57.972
or do you even play other
games outside of chess?

00:37:00.562 --> 00:37:02.972
- The thing is for something like Go,

00:37:02.972 --> 00:37:07.972
I feel like chess might
almost be (mumbles)

00:37:08.152 --> 00:37:09.622
Having spent a lot of time in chess

00:37:09.622 --> 00:37:12.812
might almost be a disadvantage

00:37:15.531 --> 00:37:20.502
because I think that the
mindset there is very different.

00:37:20.502 --> 00:37:21.732
And for a chess player,

00:37:21.732 --> 00:37:26.732
Go seems very, very
abstract compared to chess.

00:37:29.952 --> 00:37:33.865
I wouldn't say I'm great at anything else.

00:37:35.482 --> 00:37:37.392
I mean, I've tried games.

00:37:37.392 --> 00:37:42.392
Like I play poker with
my friends occasionally

00:37:42.812 --> 00:37:45.045
without being particularly great at it.

00:37:48.212 --> 00:37:51.122
I've tried, Shogi, Go briefly

00:37:51.122 --> 00:37:54.042
without excelling at any of those things.

00:37:54.042 --> 00:37:58.082
So I really wouldn't know.

00:37:58.082 --> 00:37:59.332
- I just imagined playing poker

00:37:59.332 --> 00:38:00.322
or any other game against you.

00:38:00.322 --> 00:38:02.592
And I do think you have
an intimidation factor

00:38:02.592 --> 00:38:04.292
which would play to your strengths

00:38:05.402 --> 00:38:07.439
- It goes away very quickly.

00:38:07.439 --> 00:38:09.072
(laughs loudly)

00:38:09.072 --> 00:38:10.762
- Well, I know we're out of time.

00:38:10.762 --> 00:38:12.082
I mean, first of all, thank you so much.

00:38:12.082 --> 00:38:14.172
I look forward to playing
the robot version of you,

00:38:14.172 --> 00:38:17.562
which I'm pretty sure will crush me,

00:38:17.562 --> 00:38:20.272
but I'm going to practice
with my co-conspirators

00:38:20.272 --> 00:38:21.672
that you met earlier.

00:38:21.672 --> 00:38:22.561
My older is,

00:38:22.561 --> 00:38:24.312
he's pretty decent.

00:38:24.312 --> 00:38:26.312
He's I think just gotten better than me.

00:38:28.212 --> 00:38:29.302
He gives me my run for the money.

00:38:29.302 --> 00:38:31.202
But Magnus, thank you so much.

00:38:31.202 --> 00:38:33.112
Yeah, thank you so much.

00:38:33.112 --> 00:38:36.022
Obviously, fascinating
what you've been able

00:38:36.022 --> 00:38:36.972
to accomplish in chess.

00:38:36.972 --> 00:38:38.442
But I think even more,

00:38:38.442 --> 00:38:40.652
chess as a metaphor for
life is very powerful

00:38:40.652 --> 00:38:42.602
and what you've been
able to do in the game.

00:38:42.602 --> 00:38:43.792
And just understanding

00:38:43.792 --> 00:38:45.872
a little bit of how you
think about chess and life

00:38:45.872 --> 00:38:49.522
I think has a lot of insights
that go well beyond chess.

00:38:49.522 --> 00:38:51.675
So thank you so much for joining us today.

00:38:52.652 --> 00:38:54.652
- Thank you so much for having me

00:38:54.652 --> 00:38:59.455
and all the best to all of
you in this difficult time.

00:39:00.562 --> 00:39:02.812
- Great, thank you so much Magnus.

00:39:02.812 --> 00:39:05.435
So thanks everyone for
joining today's Homeroom.

00:39:07.862 --> 00:39:10.412
I could have talked to
Magnus for a few more hours.

00:39:11.246 --> 00:39:12.352
I feel like we've got glimpses

00:39:12.352 --> 00:39:14.422
into what goes on in his mind

00:39:14.422 --> 00:39:16.622
both intellectually and emotionally.

00:39:16.622 --> 00:39:18.142
And you know, some of the takeaways,

00:39:18.142 --> 00:39:20.102
he's a human being just
like the rest of us.

00:39:20.102 --> 00:39:24.102
But it would be fun to be
able to see a chess board

00:39:24.102 --> 00:39:25.672
the way that he sees a chess board.

00:39:25.672 --> 00:39:29.202
But you can see a 10-year-old
robot version of Magnus,

00:39:29.202 --> 00:39:30.782
crushing me likely in chess.

00:39:30.782 --> 00:39:33.515
It's all about setting
expectations in the right place.

00:39:34.692 --> 00:39:38.772
We're going to have a live
event as the card had shown

00:39:38.772 --> 00:39:39.935
in a couple of days.

00:39:40.892 --> 00:39:43.312
But thank you all for joining us.

00:39:43.312 --> 00:39:46.222
It's gonna be @ChampionsChessTour.com.

00:39:46.222 --> 00:39:48.882
And I'm definitely part
of the entertainment

00:39:48.882 --> 00:39:50.832
more than the serious competition.

00:39:50.832 --> 00:39:52.392
Well, I do play a little bit.

00:39:52.392 --> 00:39:54.295
As Magnus said, it's now documented.

00:39:55.907 --> 00:39:56.740
He remembers the game,

00:39:56.740 --> 00:39:58.642
which I find incredibly flattering,

00:39:58.642 --> 00:40:01.532
but he also admitted that
I briefly had a moment

00:40:01.532 --> 00:40:02.962
when he forgot something

00:40:02.962 --> 00:40:05.172
because he was playing for
other people simultaneously

00:40:05.172 --> 00:40:06.562
with a blindfold on.

00:40:06.562 --> 00:40:07.682
But with that, thanks so much

00:40:07.682 --> 00:40:09.582
for coming to today's Homeroom

00:40:09.582 --> 00:40:12.342
and I will see you all next week, I think.

00:40:12.342 --> 00:40:13.175
See you then.

00:40:17.325 --> 00:40:19.908
(bouncy music)

